<script module>
  import Demo from "$ui/components/Demo.abide"
  import CardDemo from "$ui/demos/templating/components/CardDemo.abide"
  import ChipDemo from "$ui/demos/templating/components/ChipDemo.abide"
  import RenderPropDemo from "$ui/demos/templating/components/RenderPropDemo.abide"
  import SpreadPropsDemo from "$ui/demos/templating/components/SpreadPropsDemo.abide"
  import NamedSlotDemo from "$ui/demos/templating/components/NamedSlotDemo.abide"
  import ReactiveComponentDemo from "$ui/demos/templating/components/ReactiveComponentDemo.abide"
  import MemberTagDemo from "$ui/demos/templating/components/MemberTagDemo.abide"
  import ComponentInBlockDemo from "$ui/demos/templating/components/ComponentInBlockDemo.abide"
  import InlineScopeDemo from "$ui/demos/templating/components/InlineScopeDemo.abide"
</script>

<main class="page">
  <h1>Components</h1>
  <p class="lead">An <strong>inline component</strong> is a TitleCase <code>&#123;#component&#125;</code> taking a single props object — a reusable builder invoked as a tag <code>&lt;Name/&gt;</code>. It starts as "name some markup and use it twice" and ends as a first-class value you can pass around; every step below is that same declaration, asked to do a little more.</p>
  <Demo id="chip" title="<code>&#123;#component&#125;</code> invoked as <code>&lt;Chip/&gt;</code>" client="src/ui/demos/templating/components/ChipDemo.abide">
    <ChipDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>The whole idea: declare a builder, invoke it as a tag. TitleCase is what distinguishes it from an element — a lowercase name is a parse error, because lowercase is reserved for tags.</p>
  </aside>
  <Demo id="card" title="Props + <code>&lt;slot/&gt;</code>" client="src/ui/demos/templating/components/CardDemo.abide">
    <CardDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>Props go in as attributes and stay reactive. Whatever sits <em>between</em> the tags fills the component's <code>&lt;slot/&gt;</code> — so a component can wrap content it knows nothing about. Both stay live after hydration.</p>
  </aside>
  <Demo id="named-slot" title="Nested <code>&#123;#component&#125;</code> as a named-slot prop" client="src/ui/demos/templating/components/NamedSlotDemo.abide">
    <NamedSlotDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>One slot covers most cases; when you need two, you don't learn new syntax. A component written <em>inside</em> another's tags is passed to it as a same-named prop and invoked as a tag — named slots, falling out of the rule already in play. The leftover markup still fills the default <code>&lt;slot/&gt;</code>.</p>
  </aside>
  <Demo id="spread-props" title="<code>&#123;...obj&#125;</code> — spread into a child's props" client="src/ui/demos/templating/components/SpreadPropsDemo.abide">
    <SpreadPropsDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>And when the props are already an object, spread them: <code>&#123;...obj&#125;</code> hands every key to the child, exactly as writing each <code>name=&#123;value&#125;</code> by hand would. (On an element the same syntax sets attributes instead — see <a href="/templating/bindings#spread">bindings</a>.)</p>
  </aside>
  <Demo id="render-prop" title="A component passed as a render-prop" client="src/ui/demos/templating/components/RenderPropDemo.abide">
    <RenderPropDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>Nothing so far required a component to be a special kind of thing, and it isn't one. Pass it as a prop and let the receiver decide where to invoke it — the render-prop pattern, with no API behind it.</p>
  </aside>
  <Demo id="reactive-component" title="Reactive component — a memo-named tag <code>&lt;Current/&gt;</code>" client="src/ui/demos/templating/components/ReactiveComponentDemo.abide">
    <ReactiveComponentDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>Because it is a value, a <a href="/state">cell</a> or <a href="/memo">memo</a> can hold one. <code>&lt;Current/&gt;</code> where <code>Current</code> is a memo re-mounts whenever the derivation picks a different component — a dynamic component, again with no new syntax.</p>
  </aside>
  <Demo id="member-tag" title="A member tag — <code>&lt;item.Icon/&gt;</code>" client="src/ui/demos/templating/components/MemberTagDemo.abide">
    <MemberTagDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>Since it is a value, it can also just be a field. A tag may be a <strong>member path</strong> — <code>&lt;row.Icon/&gt;</code> — so a row carries its own renderer as data instead of the list branching on a <code>kind</code>. The head is any binding in scope; TitleCase applies to the component's <em>name</em>, which is the last segment (<code>&lt;row.icon/&gt;</code> is a parse error).</p>
    <p>And because it is an <em>expression</em> over bindings rather than an import, it is reactive for the same reason the memo-named tag above is: hand a row a different component and that one tag re-mounts. The <code>by</code> key hasn't changed, so the row's own <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> is never rebuilt — only the icon inside it. (A hyphen is what keeps a dotted tag an element: <code>&lt;my-el.foo&gt;</code> is legal HTML.)</p>
  </aside>
  <Demo id="in-block" title="A component invoked — and defined — inside <code>&#123;#for&#125;</code> / <code>&#123;#if&#125;</code>" client="src/ui/demos/templating/components/ComponentInBlockDemo.abide">
    <ComponentInBlockDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>A block body is ordinary markup, so nothing new applies: a <code>{'{'}#for{'}'}</code> invokes <code>&lt;Row/&gt;</code> once per keyed item, and a <code>{'{'}#if{'}'}</code> branch can <em>declare</em> its own component in the branch body. Keying still does what keying does — a reversed list <strong>moves</strong> each row's whole component range rather than rebuilding it, so a row that carries state keeps it.</p>
  </aside>
  <Demo id="inline-scope" title="Per-invocation <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> + <code>&lt;style&gt;</code>" client="src/ui/demos/templating/components/InlineScopeDemo.abide">
    <InlineScopeDemo />
  </Demo>
  <aside class="aside">
    <p>An inline component owns the same two things a file component does, one level in: its <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> runs <strong>per invocation</strong>, so every tally has its own count, and its <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> reaches its own subtree — the <code>.tally</code> outside it keeps the class and none of the styling.</p>
  </aside>
</main>
